DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmcchhaannggee reads a PPM image as input and changes all  pixels  of  color
       _o_l_d_c_o_l_o_r to color _n_e_w_c_o_l_o_r.

       You  may specify up to 256 oldcolor/newcolor pairs on the command line.
       ppppmmcchhaannggee leaves all colors not mentioned unchanged, unless you specify
       the --rreemmaaiinnddeerr option, in which case they are all changed to the single
       specified color.

       You can specify that colors similar, but not identical, to the ones you
       specify get replaced by specifying a "closeness" factor.

       Specify   the   colors   as   described   for   the   argument  of  the
       ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(()) library routine ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

       If a pixel matches two different _o_l_d_c_o_l_o_rs, ppppmmcchhaannggee replaces it  with
       the _n_e_w_c_o_l_o_r of the leftmost specified one.

       The  maxval of the output image is the same as that of the input image.
       If a _n_e_w_c_o_l_o_r you specify cannot be exactly represented in that maxval,
       ppppmmcchhaannggee  assumes  a  color  that  is as close as possible to what you
       specified but can be represented with your maxval.  Unless you  specify
       the --cclloosseeookk option, ppppmmcchhaannggee issues a warning that it is using an ap‐
       proximation.

       A common way that you can have this maxval problem, where the color you
       specify cannot be represented with your maxval, is that your input is a
       PBM (black and white) image that you are  colorizing.   The  maxval  in
       this  case  is  1,  which  severely  limits the colors to which you can
       change.  To avoid this problem, use ppaammddeepptthh to make the maxval of your
       input  something  consistent  with  your colors.  255 is usually a good
       choice.

       Before Netpbm 10.22 (April 2004), ppppmmcchhaannggee always behaved  as  if  the
       user specified --cclloosseeookk, and there was no --cclloosseeookk option.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppppmmcchhaannggee recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --cclloosseenneessss _c_l_o_s_e_n_e_s_s___p_e_r_c_e_n_t
              _c_l_o_s_e_n_e_s_s  is a percentage indicating how close to the color you
              specified a pixel must be to get replaced.  By default, it is 0,
              which means the pixel must be the exact color you specified.

              A  pixel  gets  replaced if the distance in color between it and
              the color you specified is less than or equal to  _c_l_o_s_e_n_e_s_s  per
              cent of the maxval.

              The  "distance"  in color is defined as the Cartesian sum of the
              individual differences in red, green, and blue  intensities  be‐
              tween  the two pixels, normalized so that the difference between
              black and white is 100%.

              This is probably simpler than what you want most the time.   You
              probably  would  like to change colors that have similar chromi‐
              nance, regardless of their intensity.  So if there's a red  barn
              that  is  variously  shadowed, you want the entire barn changed.
              But because the shadowing significantly changes  the  color  ac‐
              cording  to  ppppmmcchhaannggee's distance formula, parts of the barn are
              probably about as distant in color from other parts of the  barn
              as they are from green grass next to the barn.

              Maybe  ppppmmcchhaannggee  will  be  enhanced  some day to do chrominance
              analysis.

              This option was new in Netpbm 9.8 (September 2000).


       --cclloosseeookk
              This option affects how ppppmmcchhaannggee interprets a color you specify
              in  the  arguments.  When you specify this option, ppppmmcchhaannggee may
              use a color close to, but not the same as what you specify.  See
              the description section ⟨#description⟩  for details.

              This  option was new in Netpbm 10.22 (April 2004).  Before that,
              ppppmmcchhaannggee always behaved as if you specified this option.


       --rreemmaaiinnddeerr _c_o_l_o_r
              ppppmmcchhaannggee changes all pixels which are not of a color for  which
              you specify an explicit replacement color on the command line to
              color _c_o_l_o_r.

              An example application of this is

              ppppmmcchhaannggee --rreemmaaiinnddeerr==bbllaacckk rreedd rreedd


              to lift only the red portions from an image, or
              ppppmmcchhaannggee --rreemmaaiinnddeerr==bbllaacckk rreedd wwhhiittee || ppppmmttooppggmm


              to create a mask file for the red portions of the image.




